# rule.require.most-common-denominator

push each operation to the outermost leaf that uses it; lift it to the common ancestor the moment a second peer domain reuses it. placement, not generalization (that is `rule.prefer.wet-over-dry`)

| phase | action |
|---|---|
| create | nest at the most specific place |
| reuse by 2+ peer domains | lift to their common ancestor, now — no duplicate |

```
src/logic/advertise/invoice/lifecycle/
├── reqInvoiceCharge.ts            # used across stages → lifecycle level
├── asStripeInvoiceItem.ts         # lifted once draft/ and adjust/ both used it
├── draft/
│   ├── genInvoiceDraft.ts
│   └── syncInvoiceDraftToStripe.ts  # draft-only → stays nested
└── adjust/
    └── adjustInvoiceItemPrice.ts
```

anti-pattern: a flat `src/logic/` of 60 task-specific operations — no one can see what belongs to draft vs adjust vs charge

signal: many shared operations between two domains → they may be one domain, falsely split · few → correctly bounded, lift to the ancestor · orthogonal → a cross-cut dimension that may warrant its own domain

test: "where is the most specific place this can live?"

enforcement: an operation placed anywhere but the common ancestor of its users = blocker; flat structure with task-specific operations = blocker; speculative lift (0 reuses) = blocker; reused by 2+ peer domains yet left nested in one = blocker
forbid twin: rule.forbid.misplaced-operations
